<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Ms. Kahlo is a 37-year-old woman with chronic post-traumatic back, pelvic, and right leg pain on long-term oxycodone (60 MME a day), who asked for a same-day visit. Her duloxetine was increased to 60 mg a week ago at the rehabilitation visit, and she takes acetaminophen 1 g three times a day. Three days ago she developed a scratchy throat, then a clear runny nose, congestion, and a dry cough. Several of the students who come to her studio have had colds. Home COVID-19 antigen tests on the first and third days were negative. Her highest home temperature was 37.8 °C. Coughing fits jolt her lower back and pelvis, and the pain is worse at night, but she has kept to no more than four oxycodone tablets a day. She bought a nighttime cold and flu liquid at the pharmacy and brought it unopened to ask whether it is safe. Its label lists acetaminophen, dextromethorphan, and doxylamine. She missed one aquatic therapy session. Her oxycodone dose has not changed, and she has had no yawning, gooseflesh, stomach cramps, diarrhea, or restlessness. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Onset</td><td>Three days ago</td></tr><tr><td>Location</td><td>Throat, nose, and chest</td></tr><tr><td>Duration</td><td>Three days</td></tr><tr><td>Character</td><td>Scratchy throat, clear nasal discharge, dry cough</td></tr><tr><td>Severity</td><td>Moderate; coughing fits worsen her back and pelvic pain</td></tr><tr><td>Timing</td><td>Cough worse at night</td></tr><tr><td>Context</td><td>Colds among her students; oxycodone dose unchanged; duloxetine increased a week ago</td></tr><tr><td>Modifying factors</td><td>Warm drinks and honey help; she has not used the cold medicine she bought</td></tr><tr><td>Associated signs and symptoms</td><td>Fatigue. Denies fever above 37.8 °C, breathlessness, wheeze, ear or facial pain, vomiting, and diarrhea</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

